{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786666900,"nonce":"23JTjfbYWQhMEpZSdnAAq0v37Ek=","title":"`effectiveRetention` narrows the retention window when a read fails, and drops partitions early","message":"**What is wrong.** `Container.effectiveRetention` (`internal/app/workers.go:397-428`) is the fold `partitions.manage` calls once an hour to decide how much to keep, and its own comment states the rule in the strongest terms available in this codebase: \"⛔ EVERY FAILURE WIDENS THE WINDOW RATHER THAN NARROWING IT. An unreadable org row, an org list that errors, a settings lookup that times out — none of them may make the dropper delete MORE than it would have... the worst outcome of a broken read is that a partition lives one hour longer and the next tick drops it.\" (`:387-392`). Two paths inside the same function contradict it, and the contradiction is already named in a comment directly above them (`:369-373`, \"THE FAILURE PATH BELOW CONTRADICTS THE ⛔ ABOVE IT\"):\n\n- `:404-409` — if `c.orgs.Scopes(ctx)` errors, the function returns `rawDays, eventMonths` seeded only from `c.Config.Retention` at `:398-399` — the deployment's configured floor — instead of the settings ceiling. An org configured to the per-org maximum of 365 days (`internal/identity/domain/settings.go:209`, `KeyRawRetention: {Min: 1, Max: 365}`) sits behind a config floor of 30 days (`internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:159`, `DefaultRawRetention = 30 * 24 * time.Hour`, wired to `Config.Retention.RawPayloads` at `internal/platform/config/config.go:333`). A `Scopes()` failure on that tick returns 30, not 365 — the raw partitions are dropped roughly 335 days early, not the \"roughly 334\" first suspected (365 − 30 = 335).\n- `:410-421` — the per-org loop `continue`s on a `GetOrg` error (`:412-417`) and keeps whatever maximum the fold had already accumulated. If the failing read belongs to the org with the longest window, that org's setting never enters the fold and the returned pair is narrower than the correct answer — the same unrecoverable loss, reached a different way.\n\nThe log line on the second path is false in exactly the case that matters: `c.Logger.ErrorContext(ctx, \"partitions.manage: could not read one tenant's retention, keeping the wider window\", ...)` (`:413`) — it does not keep the wider window, it keeps whatever window it had, which is narrower whenever the unread org was the one asking for more.\n\n**The demonstration.** No repro was built; both paths are read directly. `internal/app/workers.go:404`: `scopes, err := c.orgs.Scopes(ctx)` → on error, `return rawDays, eventMonths` at `:408`, where those two locals were set at `:398-399` from `c.Config.Retention` alone — the per-org loop at `:410-426` never runs. `internal/app/workers.go:410-417`: `for _, scope := range scopes { org, err := c.Identity.GetOrg(ctx, scope); if err != nil { ...; continue } ...}` — a `continue` skips folding that org's `RawRetention`/`EventRetention` (`:420-425`) into `rawDays`/`eventMonths` for good; there is no second pass and no error return that would let a caller retry just that org.\n\n**Why it matters.** `managePartitions` runs hourly — `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:297`, `add(time.Hour, KindPartitionsManage, func() (river.JobArgs, *river.InsertOpts) { return PartitionsManageArgs{}, nil })` — and its own comment calls the mechanism out explicitly: `internal/app/workers.go:306`, \"RETENTION IS DROP PARTITION, ALWAYS. A DELETE on a thirteen-month event table is an outage.\" `oto_partitions_manage` (the SQL function it calls) drops raw and event partitions outright; there is no soft-delete and no undo. ADR 0024 confirms the loss is currently unrecoverable: cold-storage export — the one mechanism that could have made a dropped partition's data recoverable — is \"SCOPED, not built\" (`docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:162`, heading \"Cold-storage export: SCOPED, not built. Until it exists, the product says so plainly\"), and the same document's `How to overturn this` section names `Container.effectiveRetention` as the exact function this decision lives in (`:238-239`: \"'Longest wins' is one function, `Container.effectiveRetention`. Deleting it restores the previous behaviour exactly.\") and states the asymmetry driving all of this: \"Widening is free; narrowing is not. That asymmetry is why every fallback in the code widens.\" (`:244-245`). So on a deployment with any org near the 365-day bound, a single transient `Scopes()` or `GetOrg()` failure during an hourly tick can silently and permanently delete that org's raw payloads and event timeline months ahead of its configured window, and today nothing can bring the dropped rows back.\n\nThe obvious \"fix it in SQL\" alternative is also checked and is wrong, per a design note recorded alongside the two bugs (`internal/app/workers.go:377-385`): a `max()` aggregate over the raw `orgs.settings` JSONB column would miss the declarative overlay that `identity.Service.GetOrg` applies to every read — verified at `internal/identity/service/reads.go:88-94`, `org, err := s.orgs.Get(ctx, scope); ...; return org.WithDeclarative(s.declarative), nil`, with the method's own comment (`:84-87`) stating \"IT OVERLAYS THE DECLARATIVE LAYER, AND EVERY CALLER GETS THE OVERLAY... a value the screen labels 'managed by configuration' is by construction the value the hot path is using.\" A raw-column aggregate in SQL would compute a maximum over numbers a deployment's own configuration may have already overridden, so the reduce has to stay where `GetOrg` (and its overlay) is called, not move into a query.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/workers.go:397-428` (`effectiveRetention`), `:404-409` (the `Scopes()` failure path), `:410-421` (the per-org `continue`), `:413` (the false log line), `:387-392` (the \"⛔ EVERY FAILURE WIDENS\" comment), `:369-385` (the comment naming both bugs and the wrong SQL-aggregate fix), `:306` (\"RETENTION IS DROP PARTITION, ALWAYS\"); `internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:297` (hourly schedule); `internal/platform/tuning/defaults.go:159` and `internal/platform/config/config.go:333` (30-day config floor); `internal/identity/domain/settings.go:209` (365-day org ceiling); `internal/identity/service/reads.go:88-94` (the declarative overlay `GetOrg` applies); `docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:162`, `:238-239`, `:244-245` (cold storage unbuilt, the function named, the asymmetry rule).\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** The `Scopes()` failure path returns the settings ceiling — `domain.Bounds(identity.KeyRawRetention).Max` and the equivalent for event retention — instead of `c.Config.Retention`'s floor, so a tenant-list failure can no longer return a narrower answer than the widest org allowed by policy. The per-org `GetOrg` failure path either widens to that same ceiling for the tick in which it occurred, or the fold is restructured so a single unreadable org cannot leave the maximum lower than it would have been had that org been readable. Both fixes are covered by a test that asserts the returned window on a `Scopes()` error and on a `GetOrg` error is never narrower than the widest configured org, mirroring the existing `TestTheDedupeKeyHorizonIsAFloorThatOnlyWidens` pattern referenced in ADR 0024 Amendment 1. The `\"keeping the wider window\"` log line is corrected to describe what the code will then actually do.","files":null}]}